(function(){const a=document.createElement("link").relList;if(a&&a.supports&&a.supports("modulepreload"))return;for(const e of document.querySelectorAll('link[rel="modulepreload"]'))s(e);new MutationObserver(e=>{for(const t of e)if(t.type==="childList")for(const o of t.addedNodes)o.tagName==="LINK"&&o.rel==="modulepreload"&&s(o)}).observe(document,{childList:!0,subtree:!0});function n(e){const t={};return e.integrity&&(t.integrity=e.integrity),e.referrerPolicy&&(t.referrerPolicy=e.referrerPolicy),e.crossOrigin==="use-credentials"?t.credentials="include":e.crossOrigin==="anonymous"?t.credentials="omit":t.credentials="same-origin",t}function s(e){if(e.ep)return;e.ep=!0;const t=n(e);fetch(e.href,t)}})();const i=[{version:"1.12.0",title:"Workbench",notes:["Redstone: dust, torches, repeaters, comparators, redstone blocks and lamps, levers, buttons and pressure plates. Also pistons and sticky pistons, observers, note blocks you can tune, dispensers, droppers and hoppers. Doors, trapdoors, fence gates and TNT respond to power. Everything has its own recipe and is in a Redstone tab in the creative inventory. Slimeballs for sticky pistons only come from the creative inventory or commands for now.","Enchanting: an enchanting table with bookshelves, lapis and three offers, an anvil for combining, repairing and renaming, and a grindstone that takes enchantments off. There are 27 enchantments with their real effects, including Sharpness, Smite, Efficiency, Fortune, Silk Touch, Unbreaking, Mending, Protection, Feather Falling, Power and Infinity. The walking dead, parasites and dinosaurs count as undead for Smite. Enchanted items shimmer, and some chests now hold enchanted gear and books. Lapis comes from loot. Firearms cannot be enchanted. Enchanting is in Outbreak and Parasitic Dino.","Building: signs you can write on (four lines, any dye colour), item frames you can turn, and paintings with thirteen original pictures. New blocks: stained glass and panes in 16 colours, terracotta in 16 colours, clay and bricks, the sandstone and quartz families, glowstone, and the missing stone slabs, stairs, walls and variants. Quartz comes from Outbreak lab loot and the Dino quarry.","Getting around: boats in oak, birch, spruce and dark oak, with or without a chest, for two riders, and fast on ice. Rails, powered rails, detector rails and activator rails, with minecarts and chest minecarts. Vehicles are saved with the world.","Multiplayer: each world keeps the same join code, so friends can rejoin without asking. The Join screen remembers recent worlds and shows which are online. The host has a Players screen with kick, ban, game mode and an Allow Cheats for Friends switch. New commands: /list, /msg, /me, /tp to a player, /kick, /ban and /pardon. The Tab list shows ping. After a dropped connection, Rejoin brings you back. Grabs, pins, spitter acid pools and the hunt of the parasites now reach every player, not only the host."]},{version:"1.11.0",title:"Together",notes:["Multiplayer: play a world with up to 8 friends online. In any world, open the pause menu and press Open to Friends. The game gives you a six-letter code, shown in chat and on the pause menu. Friends press Multiplayer on the title screen, type the code and join. No port forwarding or setup is needed, and it works on the website and in the launcher.","The host's game runs the world, as with Open to LAN. Blocks, doors, fluids, crops, chests and furnaces are shared, along with the time, the weather and the seasons. Mobs, animals, dinosaurs, dropped items and experience are shared too. Mobs hunt whoever is nearest, and each player's hits, shields and armour count as their own. The world keeps running for your friends when the host pauses or switches tabs.","Friends see each other with name tags, walking, sneaking and swinging, holding what they hold. Chat goes to everyone, joining and leaving are announced, deaths are announced, and Tab lists everyone online. Players can hit each other. The night is skipped only when everyone is asleep, and the action bar shows how many are sleeping.","Each friend keeps their own inventory, health, experience, position and bed in the host's world, and gets them back when they rejoin. A friend's game never saves the host's world into its own world list: Save and Quit becomes Disconnect. Both sides must run the same version of the game.","Only the host can use kitchen stations, the lightroom and locked keypad lockers in a shared world."]},{version:"1.10.0",title:"Full Toolkit",notes:["Everything still missing from the Old Hands pass, so the game plays the way a long-time block-game player expects.","Game modes: Creative now has the proper creative inventory. It has item tabs with a scrollbar, Search Items, Saved Hotbars (C + 1-9 saves a row, X + 1-9 loads it) and a Survival Inventory tab with a Destroy Item slot. Clicks work the way you know: take one, shift for a stack, middle-click to clone. Adventure and Spectator modes join Survival and Creative. In Adventure you cannot break or place blocks, but you can still use doors and chests. In Spectator you fly through blocks unseen. /gamemode takes 0-3 or the names, and F3+F4 cycles all four. F3+N switches to Spectator and back. Create World cycles Survival, Hardcore and Creative, and a Hardcore death offers Spectate World.","Shields: craft one from six planks around an iron ingot. Hold right-click to raise it from either hand. Hits from the front are blocked: melee, arrows and blasts. Big hits wear it down, and melee attackers get knocked back. An axe hit knocks the shield down for 5 seconds. You walk slowly while it is raised.","Tools on blocks: right-click a log with an axe to strip it (all seven woods). A shovel on grass or dirt makes a dirt path, and a shovel on a lit campfire puts it out. A hoe tills grass, dirt and dirt paths into farmland, and turns coarse dirt into dirt. Each use costs one durability.","Buckets and shears: an empty bucket picks up water and lava, and emptying one leaves the bucket. Milk a cow with a bucket. Milk clears status effects, but not the infection or bleeding. Shears take wool off a sheep, and the wool grows back while it grazes.","Farm animals: feed two of a kind their food to breed them: wheat for cows and sheep, carrots, potatoes or beetroots for pigs, seeds for chickens. Hearts show, a baby is born and grows up in 20 minutes, and feeding it speeds that up. Animals follow you when you hold their food. Dye a sheep by using a dye on it. Lambs mix their parents' colours, and sixteen coloured wools come with it.","Crops in Outbreak and Parasitic Dino: wheat, carrots, potatoes and beetroots grow on farmland at the familiar rate. Water and light help, bone meal speeds them up, and jumping on farmland tramples it. Grass sometimes drops wheat seeds, and the loot tables and the walking dead carry seeds and vegetables. Bread, baked potatoes and red dye can be crafted. The Wilderness keeps its own seasonal farming.","Beds: sixteen beds, one per wool colour, in every mode. Using one sets your respawn point. At night, with no monsters close, you lie down, the screen fades and you wake up in the morning with the weather cleared. Esc, sneaking, Leave Bed or being hurt wakes you early. Wilderness and Parasitic Dino bedrolls sleep the same way.","Subtitles: Accessibility Settings has Show Subtitles. Sounds you can hear get a short caption at the bottom right, with an arrow toward where they come from. Each caption fades out over a few seconds.","Two chests side by side are now drawn as one Large Chest, with one wide lid that opens as a single piece.","Graphics: new players start on a plain vanilla look (Vanilla+). The richer presets are still in the Realism Preset option, and a preset you already chose is kept."]},{version:"1.9.0",title:"Old Hands",notes:["A pass over everything a long-time block-game player would trip on, so the game behaves the way your hands expect.",'Controls: pressing Esc twice no longer leaves you back in the world with a free mouse and a frozen player. If the browser will not hand the mouse back, the game stays paused behind "Click to return to game". Any mouse button takes the mouse back, and that click does nothing else. Sprinting with Ctrl no longer sets off browser shortcuts: Ctrl+D, Ctrl+S and the rest are ignored in a world. Closing the tab while playing asks first, and in fullscreen Ctrl+W and Esc reach the game.',"Controls: hotbar keys work while sneaking. Key binds follow the key position, so other keyboard layouts work. Rebound inventory, drop, swap and hotbar keys also work inside the inventory. The arrow keys no longer move you. There is a new Toggle Cinematic Camera bind, unbound by default.","Chat: Tab completes commands and their arguments. /gamemode (and /gm, 0 and 1) switches between Survival and Creative, and F3+F4 does the same. /gamerule sets keepInventory. The F3 screen shows the light level and the targeted entity. F3+B draws hitboxes on every mob and dropped item.","Inventory: shift-double-click moves every stack of that item at once. Two chests side by side open as one Large Chest. In a crafting table, shift-click puts items into the grid first. A right-click followed by a quick left-click is no longer taken as a double-click. Drags start from any press, and only slots that can take the item join in. Results fill the hotbar from the right, as in the original. The furnace output tops up a held stack. Container screens close when you walk more than 8 blocks away.","Crafting: crafting and stonecutter outputs show proper tooltips. A number key crafts straight into that hotbar slot, Q drops one craft, and Ctrl+Q crafts and drops as many as you can. The recipe book only lists recipes you have unlocked and counts the items in the grid. Clicking a recipe again adds another set, and a recipe you cannot make shows its ingredients greyed out in the grid. The book starts closed and remembers whether you opened it. Advanced tooltips (F3+H) are remembered.","Building: using a door, trapdoor, lever or chest no longer places your held block a moment later. Holding right-click on a door keeps using it. Holding right-click places at the original rate. The off hand is used when the main hand has nothing to do: torches, blocks, food and throwables. Snow layers are replaced when you build on them. Sneaking with empty hands still opens doors and chests. Pick block uses a free hotbar slot instead of pushing your tool out.","Creative: placing and throwing use nothing up, you reach mobs 5 blocks away, and the bow fires without arrows.","Death: outside the Wilderness, dying drops all your items and some experience where you fell, unless /gamerule keepInventory true is set. Hardcore death keeps the world: the screen offers Title Screen and never deletes it. The red pulsing screen at low health is gone. Food gives saturation from its own value, so eating when nearly full still fills saturation.","Saving: the world autosaves every 5 minutes and when you pause, instead of every half minute while building. Only chunks changed since the last save are written. Opening the inventory no longer saves. Save and Quit lands on Select World. Statistics are kept with the world: deaths, distances, time played, items crafted and nights survived, plus Time Since Last Death.","Items are picked up while a screen is open. F1 hides the chat and the saving label too. Infection messages in the action bar are no longer swallowed. Toasts slide in and out with a soft sound.","Options: new Chat Settings (text opacity, background opacity, size, delay), Notification Time, Tutorial Hints, and an Autosave Indicator switch. The tutorial plays once per player, not in every new world, and only mentions the infection where there is one. Master and Music volume start at 100% for new players."]},{version:"1.8.8",title:"Hold to Drop",notes:["Holding Q keeps dropping the held item one at a time, like Minecraft, until you let go. Each drop swings the arm, and Ctrl+Q held drops a whole stack each time. The same works with the mouse over a slot in an inventory screen: hold Q to keep dropping from that stack.","Q pressed while a screen is open never drops anything later, after the screen closes. The Handheld GPS still never drops, however long Q is held."]},{version:"1.8.7",title:"Field Dressing",notes:["Wilderness: a killed animal goes limp, falls over on its side and stays there as a carcass instead of dropping its meat. Hold H with a Buck Knife while looking at it to clean it. A bar under the crosshair fills, and the arm cuts with a sound and a drop of blood. Then the meat, hide and the rest of its drops come out. A bigger animal takes longer: a duck about 4 seconds, a deer about 6, a bison about 8 and a half. If you let go, your progress on that carcass is kept. An animal that died burning gives cooked meat. A carcass nobody cleans rots away after 10 minutes. Carcasses and their progress are saved with the world.","Every Wilderness world, new or old, hands you a Buck Knife once. It is an iron knife with 250 uses that also cuts grass for fibre and shears. You can craft another from an iron ingot over a stick over leather. Fish caught on a line, and animals without meat, drop as before.","Wilderness: every survivor starts with a Handheld GPS. Hold it in either hand to read your block position, heading, elevation and a waypoint. Use it to mark a waypoint, and sneak-use to clear it. It never leaves your inventory. You can move it to any slot or the off hand, but it cannot be dropped, thrown, put in a chest, backpack or crafting grid, or lost on death.","Fixed: breaking a block with one click, and dropping an item with Q, did not swing the arm. In the inventory screen, holding Q over an item no longer keeps dropping it: one press drops one.","Look-alike item icons are redrawn so they are easy to tell apart. Berries are a red sprig with leaves, and coffee cherries grow on a twig. Every meat has its own cut: a beef steak with a fat cap, a bear T-bone, a bison rib rack, a boar ham, a rolled roast and a patty. Bass, catfish and fish slices have different bodies, and the long loaf and the round barley loaf are different breads. Each soup and stew has its own broth in the bowl, and each seed has its own shape and colour. Salted food shows clear salt grains. The Buck Knife and the GPS have their own icons.","The field journal has a new page, Field Dressing, which opens on your first kill."]},{version:"1.8.6",title:"Gloomier Infection",notes:["The purple parasite blocks no longer look like candy. Biomass, infested ground, flesh, spore nodes, nest hearts, the stalks, pods and hanging tendrils, and the infection on dirt, stone, grass and lab tiles are repainted in a gloomy key: dark bruise-plum growth instead of bright magenta and pastel pink, dried dark meat, grimy mottling and pits, dull ochre pus and a dim cold glow instead of lime beads and near-white cyan.","Infested ground lost the bright bead and fleck that repeated on every block like confetti, and the lobes lost their shiny cartoon highlights.","Infected zones are hazed in a grey-plum murk instead of a bright blue-violet, and the drifting spores are ashen instead of glowing cyan and lilac."]},{version:"1.8.5",title:"Blocky Wildlife",notes:["All 33 Wilderness animals are block-game mobs now, like the farm animals in 1.8.4: a few chunky boxes each with flat pixel-art skins instead of realistic bodies. Bears with a shoulder hump, shaggy bison, deer with branched antlers, a red wolf with amber eyes, a masked raccoon, a skunk with its tail up, songbirds you can tell apart by colour, a black and white orca, a lobster with big orange claws, a snail with a banded shell, and the rest.","They walk with real leg swings, graze with their heads down, lie down to sleep, call, flap and glide, swim and flop on land, and their eyes still blink and follow you. Sheep can still be sheared, attacks still land on the strike, and the ragdolls still fall the same way.","Fixed: once an animal had attacked, it never flinched when hit or called again for the rest of its life. The skunk now stamps with its tail straight up before it sprays."]},{version:"1.8.4",title:"Blocky Farm Animals",notes:["Pigs, cows, sheep and chickens are block-game mobs again instead of realistic animals: a few chunky boxes each (a square pig with a flat snout and floppy ears, a brown and cream cow with short horns, a woolly sheep, a white hen with a red comb), flat light pixel-art skins, and their legs really walk now (the old models mostly slid along with a sway).","They graze with their heads down to the grass, lie down with their legs folded to sleep, lift their heads to call, tip over on their side when they die, and the hen flaps her wings when she drops from a height. Outbreak's assimilated animals use the new bodies too."]},{version:"1.8.3",title:"Scarier Zombies",notes:['Zombie voices are remade. Their groans were sung vowels whose pitch rose in the middle, a goofy "uuh?". They are now throat sounds: low growls that sink, rattling breaths drawn in, wet gurgling, snarls through bared teeth, and harsher hurt and death sounds that end in a choking rattle.',"Fixed: a zombie's groan pitch was randomised twice, so some came out squeaky. The Mutant Giant now roars from where it stands, deeper, instead of from the nearest zombie."]},{version:"1.8.2",title:"Smoother Ultra",notes:["Ultra shaders run about twice as fast on integrated graphics. On a Radeon 780M at 4K, Ultra took over 10 ms of GPU time a frame, so Auto resolution sat at its lowest step and still missed frames (50 to 70 fps on a 120 Hz screen). It now takes about 5 ms, and the game holds 120 fps at a higher resolution.","How: when the sun moves, its shadow map is redrawn a quarter at a time over four frames instead of all at once, and a little less often; mobs only put back the part of the map they covered; solid blocks get a cheaper shadow pass; light shafts read a small copy of the map; and the screen-effect layer leaves the page while it shows nothing. Shadows and light shafts look the same.","Sharper picture with Auto resolution: when the game renders below your screen size and the stretch is not an exact 2x, the image is sharpened (contrast-adaptive sharpening) so block edges and textures stay crisp instead of going soft."]},{version:"1.8.1",title:"Loading Screen",notes:["Opening the game in the browser now shows a loading screen while it downloads (about 1 MB), with a timer and a note when the connection is slow, instead of a dark page with only a crosshair that looked broken.","If the game files fail to download, the page says so and offers Reload; if the title screen cannot open, the error dialog explains why instead of leaving the page blank."]},{version:"1.8.0",title:"Golden Hour",notes:["Golden Hour (Video Settings > Realism, on in Realism 4 and Cinematic): at sunrise and sunset the low sun keeps lighting the land, warm and amber with long shadows, instead of the world going flat and grey. Shade keeps its usual dusk light. Warm Low Sun can be turned off on its own.","Light Shafts (same page, also on in Realism 4 and Cinematic): rays of sunlight past trees, hills and cloud towers when you look toward the sun, strongest at sunrise and sunset. They fade out in rain, underground and under water, and cost nothing when you look away or at night. Shaft Strength sets how strong they are.","Fixes: the sunset glow along the horizon no longer ends in a hard vertical edge to the side of the sun. Long notes in the Realism menus keep to the button column instead of running under the scroll bar."]},{version:"1.7.8",title:"Loose Ends",notes:["Arrows and ice balls hit animals with vanilla damage (they hit them 2.5x too hard); parasites and dinosaurs, whose health is on a larger scale, are unchanged.","A thrown weapon or arrow still in the air when the world saves is kept as an item where it was, with its wear and oil. Status causes survive a reload. Rotten flesh in Wilderness costs 2 hydration and always brings 30 s of Thirst, as planned.","Outbreak: kills count for the whole world, not just one life (the death screen still shows this life). Running generators, a damaged mini-boss and its rage are saved. The reserve count beside the magazine counts only the ammo a reload would use.","Wilderness and Parasitic Dino worlds no longer show Outbreak weapon key binds, credits or changelog notes. The infection and bleeding vignette no longer tints open screens, Enter saves in Edit World, long toast titles stay inside their frame, and turning off reduced motion in your system settings now brings back the default damage tilt and title animation."]},{version:"1.7.7",title:"Bug Hunt II",notes:["Screens: blindness, frost and heat overlays, the boss bar, effect icons, the season label and the body figure no longer draw over the inventory and other screens. Slot and recipe-book icons redraw when the GUI scale changes. Wide tooltips stay on screen. Slowness has its own effect icon.","The offhand item is shown in a slot left of the hotbar, as in Minecraft. A held block is no longer full-bright at night or in caves.","Menus: Create New World scrolls, so every option can be reached; the last world in a long list is no longer hidden; worlds show their mode and the version they were last played in. Esc on a journal or the Dino ending no longer opens the Game Menu over it, chat no longer opens over paused screens, toasts fit titles that wrap, a world hidden by the search can no longer be deleted, and Hardcore worlds cannot allow commands.","Wilderness: thirst no longer kills on Easy or Normal (it stops where hunger does). New kitchen sinks fill with rain, knives and shears wear when shearing, snake poison no longer gets through a block or shield, a carried animal keeps its coat, and nausea is saved. The far sea is no longer frozen or glowing outside winter.","Outbreak: explosions no longer open sealed goal rooms, nest anchors and hearts destroyed by explosions, fire or cleansing now count, and a hive stops breeding once its heart is gone. Removing a magazine attachment with a full inventory drops the extra rounds instead of deleting them. Sentry turrets leave farm animals alone, the supply drop message points the right way, Frostbites despawn, two of the same weapon show their own magazine, and the Surgeon and Well Armed advancements work. No horde warning on Peaceful.","Parasitic Dino: Firepot fires and flares burn out even far away, bleeding is saved, and /kill zombies no longer kills dinosaurs.","Commands: answers and help match the world's mode, /give rejects odd names and negative counts, /set health 0 kills, /time and /weather reject bad values, and the bow no longer uses arrows or wears out in creative."]},{version:"1.7.6",title:"Sharper Icons",notes:["Item icons in the inventory, hotbar and every other screen are drawn at the exact size your screen shows them, with smoothed edges and curves (Scale4x), so they look crisp instead of blocky, on 4K screens too.","Block icons (logs, planks, ores, stone...) no longer look jagged: they are drawn at your screen's resolution instead of being shrunk from a fixed size.","Redrawn icons: axes of every tier (a proper blade and socket), dyes (little tied pouches in each colour), redstone, glowstone, gunpowder, blaze powder and bone meal piles, and coal."]},{version:"1.7.5",title:"Small Fixes",notes:["Dropped items are now saved with the world: leave and come back and they are still on the ground. As in Minecraft, an item disappears after 5 minutes on the ground, and that clock (and its motion) only runs while the area around it is loaded, so walking away and back does not make it vanish early.","Falling sand and gravel are saved too, and a block falling into an unloaded area waits there instead of being lost.","Thirst no longer hurts on Peaceful: it refills on its own, like hunger.","Outbreak: attachments fitted at the Gunsmith go on the gun in its slot, not on another gun of the same kind in your hand, and a gun's tooltip lists its own attachments.","Closing a note or journal page captures the mouse again, so you can look around straight away.","/season set always lands on day 1 of the chosen season (at some hours it showed the last day of the season before)."]},{version:"1.7.4",title:"Bug Hunt",notes:["Fixed item duplication: picked-up drops (mob loot, campfire cooking, airdrops, handed-back items) no longer reappear twice, shift-clicking a craft into a nearly full inventory no longer gives free items, and a carried stack no longer grows past 64.","Fixed item loss: whole stacks swapped into a brewing bottle slot, items on the cursor or in the crafting grid when the game saves, and empty bottles with a full inventory are no longer lost. The recipe book no longer repairs tools or freshens food.","Wilderness: frontier weapons and flint and copper tools now hit as hard as they should (they hit like a bare fist). Body temperature, a carried block or animal, and items on the cursor no longer carry over a death. Skunks no longer spray on Peaceful. Kitchen sinks keep their water when it rains while you are away. Cold-sea ice no longer pops into water in front of you in spring.","Parasitic Dino: big dinosaurs no longer walk inside hills, creative players are no longer hunted or pinned, fires crackle and the rescue helicopter is heard again, and a running generator survives a reload.","Outbreak: night hordes and called parasites despawn when you are far away instead of piling up, farm animals no longer use up the parasite cap, the grace period and the Survivor advancement no longer count paused time, ticks let go when you die, a fresh gun is no longer broken because another of its kind wore out, and repair kits, multi-tools, flashlights and notes can be used with right click.","Sleeping after midnight no longer turns the day counter back, and a double click on a bedroll no longer skips two days.","Fire, a drawn bow and eating progress no longer carry over a death or to another item. Typing q, r or f in chat no longer drops, reloads or swaps, the mouse wheel no longer changes slots behind open screens, and Toggle Sneak survives Shift in a screen.","Saving: a death is saved at once (Hardcore stays one life even if the tab closes), a failed save warns before quitting, a save asked for during another save is no longer dropped, and Edit World no longer undoes a restored backup. Loading a world far from spawn is about a second faster.","The pause screen freezes water, leaves and supply drops, the Dino wind goes quiet, and the jukebox follows its own volume slider. /tp and /set reject impossible numbers that froze the game. The landing page no longer scrolls sideways on phones."]},{version:"1.7.3",title:"Small Fixes",notes:["Outbreak: a new world no longer starts in sight of a Parasite Hive or another Danger 3+ site. The spawn keeps at least 64 blocks away, and nothing is announced as discovered the moment you join."]},{version:"1.7.2",title:"New Item Art",notes:["Every item icon is redrawn by hand at 16 x 16: tools, weapons, armor, food, drinks, crops, materials, gear, guns and ammo. Cleaner shapes, richer shading and light from the top-left, so items read at a glance in your inventory and hotbar. Block items are unchanged."]},{version:"1.7.1",title:"Small Fixes",notes:["New Wilderness worlds start in the open wild with empty hands: no spawn tower, no structures and no chests anywhere. Loose rocks sometimes hold a flint for your first tools. Wilderness worlds made before keep their tower and structures.","Wilderness lakes and rivers only freeze in winter; ice over water thaws in the other seasons.","Taking items from a chest no longer floods the screen with recipe toasts: you get one, after the chest closes.","Advancements follow the mode: Wilderness starts with Into the Wild and Parasitic Dino with Lost World, and parasite and gun advancements only show in Outbreak."]},{version:"1.7",title:"The Far Horizons Update",notes:["Cinematic preset: clouds rise into stepped voxel towers you can fly through, lakes reflect the clouds above them, and sunlight ripples across the floor under water, with softer shore foam. Auto and Realism 4 look and run as before.","Far Distance (Video Settings > Realism, off by default): coarser far terrain out to 192 chunks. Blocks you break or place show up in the far terrain straight away, and the structures you have built keep their shape far away.","Mob Ragdolls (Video Settings > Realism, off by default): a killed mob, animal or dinosaur goes limp for about 2 seconds before it fades. Dinosaurs fall on their own necks and tails.","Sound bends around corners and through open doors, so you hear a mob from the doorway instead of through the wall. New in Music & Sounds > Realism Sounds: Positional Audio (Stereo or HRTF for headphones), reverb and absorption settings. New ambience by biome, at night, in rain and thunder, and a Parasitic Dino forest by day and by night.","Outbreak security devices: the Code Keypad, Retinal Scanner, Inventory Scanner, Cage Trap, Sentry, Motion-Activated Light and Reinforced Iron Bars. Devices belong to the player who placed them.","Gun packs: high-poly gun models now load, and a pack can bring its own languages. Pick one in Options > Language to see its gun names and descriptions translated.","Outbreak parasites merge: a group of the same kind gathers, shudders and grows into one stronger form. Hit one of them in time to break it up. Turn it off on the World tab.","Desktop launcher 1.4: themes, custom skins, installation sharing.","Cinematic now also turns on Far Distance (64 chunks) and Mob Ragdolls, and its cloud towers have rounded, stepped tops. You can still change either setting yourself.","Custom skins show their second layer (hat, jacket, sleeves, trousers) and slim 3-pixel arms, on your body in third person and on your hand in first person. The launcher detects slim arms from the skin file, and the Skin tab's Arms button can change that.","Gun packs: a gun with no English name now shows a name from one of the pack's other languages instead of its raw key. Options > Language only appears when a loaded pack has a language other than English.","Launcher: the Default and Dark themes have clearer button text and controls."]},{version:"1.6.2",title:"Small Fixes",notes:["Desktop launcher 1.3.1: newer engine, smoother Parasitic Dino.","The Quetzalcoatlus no longer overshoots its dives."]},{version:"1.6.1",title:"Smoother Frames",notes:["Higher, steadier frame rates on integrated graphics: nearby leaves and plants are lit once per pixel, the sky is no longer drawn behind terrain, and Auto resolution leaves room for your desktop. On a 120 Hz screen the launcher now holds 120 in Outbreak and Wilderness. Nothing looks different.","Fixed: a click made during a short frame hitch was sometimes ignored, so a chest or door did not open."]},{version:"1.6",title:"The Overgrown Park Update",notes:["Wilderness Survival can now be picked in Create New World (World tab, Mode): no monsters and no guns, four seasons, cold, thirst and hunger, 33 wild animals, and tools you knap, forge and smith yourself. The Play now buttons on the website start a new world in the mode you picked.","Smoother on desktop: the launcher no longer drops most of the frames it draws on Linux, so it now shows about 90-110 frames a second on a 120 Hz screen where it showed under 20.","Parasitic Dino worlds are one giant sequoia forest: huge reddish-brown trunks twice as tall as before, a high canopy, ferns and podzol underfoot, and vines on the trunks you can climb. There is no ice or snow any more. Falling trees still work with the taller trunks.","The old labs are now an abandoned, overgrown dinosaur park: a visitor centre, a vet station, a maintenance shed, broken paddocks, feeding stations, picnic areas, signposts and a tour road with wrecked cars built from blocks. Every structure is taken over by the forest. The story notes are gone; the field journal species pages stay.","Dinosaurs look infected from a distance, with moving tentacles, growths and exposed flesh, and they move with new walk, run and attack animations.","Dinosaur territories are far apart, and only one dinosaur (or one small pack) ever chases you at a time. Infected plant-eaters no longer flee: they guard their ground and charge, swing, club, headbutt or stomp.","Dinosaurs reach as far as their bodies do, keep hunting until they truly lose you, follow where they last saw you and find paths around cliffs. Pterosaurs circle and dive again. Hitboxes fit the body, head, tail and wings closely.","Microraptors perch, Compsognathus gather at your body, small species can have more than one group, and hive parasites can be hit with arrows and thrown items.","Repainted blocks (labs and the most common terrain), redrawn item icons that stay crisp at every GUI scale, and reworked purple parasite blocks that look wet, layered and infected."]},{version:"1.5",title:"The Realism Update",notes:["Shaders Lite (Realism 4): soft bloom on bright light, a gentle colour grade that follows the biome and the infection, and softer shadows. Grass and leaves sway in the wind and bend as you walk through them. Passable Leaves is a new world option.","Sound you can place: sounds behind walls are muffled, rooms and caves echo, and five ambience beds (wind, birds, insects, caves and the Outbreak infection hum) follow the time of day and where you stand.","Far terrain: one ring of distant hills, with their trees, past your render distance. Breaking a block throws small debris cubes that land and fade.","Cinematic preset (Video Settings > Realism, opt-in, for strong graphics cards): material highlights and wet gloss on 30 blocks, far shadows, ambient occlusion, reflections on water and puddles, a scattering sky, lit clouds with cloud shadows, gusting wind and better lit mobs. Vanilla+ and Realism 4 look as before.","Outbreak gunsmithing: the Gunsmith Workbench fits scopes, muzzles, grips and magazines to your guns (nine attachments, with a stat panel showing what each one changes) and crafts attachments and ammo.","Parasites learn: hit them the same way for long enough and they resist it, more at higher evolution phases. Switch between guns, melee, fire and explosives. Screamers call for reinforcements; interrupt the call or more arrive from the dark. Bites can infect you, and the Antiparasitic Serum cures it.","Lab security: Security Cameras and a Monitor to look through them, Keycard Readers that open iron doors for a card of their level, and four reinforced blocks that parasites cannot break.","Outbreak structures look lived in and ruined: wear and stains, props that point toward the goal and the generator, roof cracks above cave-ins, ceiling lights that stay dark until the power is back, red alarm lamps, their own ambient sounds, and masts, smoke and floodlights you can see from far away.","Desktop launcher 1.2: a Dark theme, a moving panorama or a solid colour background, per-installation game options (realism preset, render distance, shaders, FPS counter) and a Packs screen for resource packs and gun packs."]},{version:"1.4",title:"The Survival Update",notes:["Every control can be rebound, mouse buttons included: attack, use, pick block, and the new Aim Weapon and Fire Weapon binds (hold the left button to aim, right to fire, as before). Clashing binds show red with the other action named; Escape unbinds a key.","Outbreak guns, rebuilt as one system: semi, burst and automatic fire (switch with V), bolt and pump actions, tactical and empty reloads from the ammo you carry, shotguns that load shell by shell, recoil, bullet drop, headshots and armour. Scopes, red dots and iron sights each aim their own way; scroll to zoom a scope. Inspect your gun with H.","Gun packs: add TACZ-format gun packs in Options > Gun Packs. Their guns, models, sounds and animations join Outbreak beside the built-in guns.","The infection evolves. Outbreak worlds climb from phase 0 to phase 5 as parasites are killed, spread and assimilate; each phase spreads faster and sends stronger mobs. Spore nodes seed it, and light level 8 or more stops it. Pick the speed (or turn it off) on the World tab.","Fight back with the Purifier, which protects and cleans the ground around it, with fire on infected ground and with the Potion of Cleansing. Parasites now hunt farm animals and turn them into Assimilated Pigs, Cows, Sheep and Chickens.","The Outbreak Journal (J) explains every phase and lists the sites you have found.","Structures have danger tiers from 1 to 5, and every tier 3 or higher site hides a goal room with a signature reward. A Survey Chart marks every site within 320 blocks.","New Outbreak worlds make you earn it: access chains, alarms that bring growing waves, three mini-bosses (the Breach Sergeant, the Shackled and the Brood Matron), a flooded vault to drain, mine cave-ins, lab missions and nest hearts. Mobs no longer break into goal rooms.","Realism settings (Video Settings > Realism and Music & Sounds > Realism Sounds): held and dropped torches and burning things light the world, water has depth colour, shore foam and splashes, and footsteps sound different on every surface. Pick a preset or leave it on Auto.","Worlds can be exported to one .dlworld file and imported again from Select World. A world saved by a newer build is refused with the version that can open it.","Desktop launcher 1.1: installations, version installs that are checked before they run, Settings, Diagnostics, a crash screen with a report that stays on your computer, offline play, and updates for the launcher and the game. Worlds from launcher 1.0 are copied to disk automatically, and the old copy is kept.","Fixed: a quick tap on an automatic gun now always fires a round, and /give accepts a target first (/give @s <item>)."]},{version:"1.3",title:"The Basin Update",notes:["Outbreak mobs rebuilt: all twelve types, the Frostbite young, the mutant giant and the Plague Heart have new, more detailed models with breathing, twitching and glowing eyes. Their health, speed, reach and hit areas are exactly as before.","Prefer the old look? Options > Video Settings > Outbreak Mobs: Classic. The change applies to mobs already in the world at once.","Parasitic Dino: two pterosaurs join the basin. The Quetzalcoatlus circles high over its home and drops down when it spots you in the open; the Hatzegopteryx stalks on all fours and takes short flights.","New Parasitic Dino worlds have four more sites: a hatchery lab with a sealed vault, the bone pit the Gravemaw calls home, a flooded quarry and lava-tube caves with a survey camp.","Fire keeps young dinosaurs back; a big fire turns even the Gravemaw. Throw a Firepot, carry a torch, but a light also shows you at night.","Rattle Bottles draw dinosaurs away, Fern Musk hides your scent and shortens their sight, and Hand Flares now light up the ground around them.","Sleep in a bedroll to wake there after you die.","A way out: power the outpost radio, repair it with a Radio Valve and an Antenna Lead, call for pickup and survive until the helicopter reaches the landing zone. You can keep playing afterwards.","Dinosaurs no longer get stuck walking into a tree trunk; they step around it."]},{version:"1.2",title:"The Modes Update",notes:["Game modes: pick one with the new Mode button on the World tab of Create World. Existing worlds stay Outbreak, unchanged.","New mode, Parasitic Dino: a foggy prehistoric forest with near-black nights, no guns, and dinosaurs taken over by a parasite called the Graft.","The Gravemaw, an infected Tyrannosaurus, hunts by sound, sight and the smell of blood. It stalks from cover, freezes when you look at it and leaves when it loses you.","A Ceratosaurus and a Spinosaurus that swims, each one individual in the world. The Graft grows through four stages and changes how each one behaves.","Abandoned survey sites to search: a dig site, an overrun outpost with a quarantine wing behind a generator, nest grounds, a crashed supply flight and a ranger station.","Field Bandages stop bleeding; Hand Flares draw dinosaurs away from you.","Each mode keeps to its own content: no item, recipe, loot or command from one mode turns up in another, and saves drop any that do.","Leaves are see-through again in Fancy graphics; Fast graphics fills the gaps with a dark leaf colour instead of grey.","Higher frame rates on fast screens. Landing in shallow water no longer hurts.","The inventory, chests and chat no longer pause the world. Wearables can be worn.","Fixed: some chests and recipes named items that do not exist, so those entries never appeared. Rope, bones and leather now turn up where they should, oak leaves drop apples and Hardtack is made from bread."]},{version:"1.1",title:"The Outbreak Update",notes:["New worlds have a visible outbreak near spawn: infection spreads in four stages, from spores to a biomass core.","Every block, item and mob redrawn in one style; parasite hosts are blockier, bloodier and scarier.","Twelve mob types and a boss, each with its own behaviour; some break blocks to reach you.","A very rare boss, the Plague Heart, rises at night with a boss bar. It drops the Zombie Heart.","Brew a Zombie Heart into an Awkward Potion for a Potion of Cleansing: the only way to clean infected blocks. Add gunpowder for a splash version.",'Placing a block above the build limit keeps the block and says "Height limit for building is 64".',"Mobs notice you less from far above or below.","Twenty guns: hold attack to aim, use to fire. Ammo count on the hotbar.","Recipe book with a Craftable filter that shows what you are missing.","New menus, one pixel font, sharper HUD at every window size, keyboard focus on every control.","First-join tutorial hints. Help text follows your key bindings.","World generation and chunk building moved to background workers: smoother while travelling, faster world open.","Video settings all do what they say; graphics tier is picked on first launch.","Saves: block palette stored with each world, automatic backup before any migration.","Removed: vehicles, NPC missions, the old shooter HUD, stamina, thirst and body temperature.","Supply drops now arrive every 30 minutes.","Worlds created from now on grow hanging tendrils from cave ceilings and overhangs in the heart of an outbreak.",'Picking up a new ingredient shows "New Recipes Unlocked!".',"Fixed: with Toggle Sneak or Toggle Sprint on, shift-clicking in a chest or crafting table left you crouched and unable to sprint.","Very quick key taps (like R to reload) are no longer missed.","New player hurt sound and new zombie groans, hurt and death sounds, all made from scratch for DEADLOCKED.","New desktop launcher for Windows, macOS and Linux with no frame-rate cap. The website has a new home page; the game is at /play/."]},{version:"1.0",title:"First release",notes:["Editable voxel world with caves, ores, biomes, weather, day and night.","Survival: mining, crafting, smelting, farming, chests, hunger.","Infected hosts, research labs and guarded structures."]}],h="Field Journal",l=["Every animal in the basin is one of a kind. There is one of each, never two, and every one carries the Graft.","Write down what you see. A page fills in the first time you see an animal, and again each time you see it further gone.","The Graft goes in stages: Grafted (pale threads at the wounds), Veined (threads under the skin), Splitting (growths breaking through) and Overgrown (more growth than animal).","When one dies, another of its kind comes back to the same ground after a few days. The page stays."],d={tyrannosaurus:{about:"The big one. The station people called it the Gravemaw. Twelve metres from snout to tail, a head as long as I am tall, and it walks without a sound until it wants you to hear it.",habits:"It listens before it looks. Noise brings it, blood brings it, and once it hunts it charges in a straight line and goes through trees as if they were grass. Stone and planks stop it. Big fires turn it away. Hurt it badly enough and it backs off to heal.",home:"Everywhere, but it dens in the darkest forest, far from where I started.",stages:["Never seen it clean. It was already Veined the first time.","Threads under the hide along the neck and flanks, pulsing when it breathes. Its eyes catch the light wrong.","Growths have split the skin over its back and jaw. It bleeds and does not seem to notice.","More Graft than animal now. The growths move on their own. It still hunts like the animal it was."]},ceratosaurus:{about:"A lean hunter with a blade of horn on its nose and bony ridges over the eyes. Smaller than the big one, quicker than it looks.",habits:"It bites and pulls back, then circles. The bite opens you up: bleeding leaves a trail it follows for minutes. Bandage the wound and the trail stops.",home:"Forest edges and river valleys.",stages:["Pale threads crusted in the bite scars along its flanks.","Threads under the skin from horn to hip. It circles closer than it used to.","Growths through the shoulders. It hisses when it breathes.","Its horn is buried in growth. It does not stop circling."]},spinosaurus:{about:"A long-snouted fisher with a tall sail on its back, built for the water more than the land. The sail tip is often the only part you see.",habits:"It waits under the surface and lunges at anything at the edge. It follows along the bank but never more than a dozen blocks inland. Water is not safe; the shore is not safe either.",home:"The biggest river, lake or swamp near its ground.",stages:["Threads around the snout and down the sail spines.","Veins of Graft through the sail, dark against the light.","Growths have torn through the sail. It still swims fast.","The sail is more growth than skin. Ripples move ahead of it now."]},hatzegopteryx:{about:"A pterosaur with a skull like a wedge and a neck as thick as a log. It stalks on all fours more than it flies.",habits:"It crosses clearings and open hills with its head low, then stabs and grabs with the beak. When it loses you it flies to the next clearing. Stay under trees.",home:"Clearings and open hills.",stages:["Threads along the wing membranes, pale against the brown.","Veins of Graft through the wings and the long neck.","Growths have opened holes in the wings. It still flies.","The wings drag growth behind them. It walks more than it flies."]},quetzalcoatlus:{about:"The tallest thing that ever flew: on the ground it stands as tall as a giraffe, all neck and beak.",habits:"It soars over open ground looking down. If it sees you in the open it lands a little way off, its shadow and wingbeats first, and walks you down. The forest canopy is cover.",home:"Coasts, open plains and the sky over them.",stages:["Threads at the joints of the wings.","Graft veins along the neck, visible from the ground when it banks.","Growths through the wing fingers. Its shadow looks wrong.","Overgrown. It still soars. I do not know how."]},allosaurus:{about:"A big hunter with short horns over the eyes and long, grasping arms. Heavier than the horned one, lighter than the big one.",habits:"It waits just inside the treeline, rushes out, slashes and goes back into the trees. Then it circles and does it again. Keep away from forest edges, or keep your back to open ground.",home:"Forest edges and the treelines around clearings.",stages:["Threads at the corners of the jaw.","Veins of Graft over the skull and down the arms.","Growths through its back. It waits longer before it rushes.","Overgrown. It hardly hides in the trees any more."]},carnotaurus:{about:"Thick horns over the eyes, tiny arms, long legs. Built for running in a straight line.",habits:"It charges across open ground, faster than anything else in the basin by the end of the run. It cannot turn. Step aside at the last moment and it skids past.",home:"Open land, wide clearings and roads.",stages:["Threads in the scale rows along its sides.","Graft veins around the horns and down the legs.","Growths through the hips. Its charges run longer.","Overgrown. It still runs. It still cannot turn."]},utahraptor:{about:"A big feathered raptor, taller than a man at the hip, with a sickle claw on each foot.",habits:"It watches from cover, climbs ledges, comes from behind and pounces. Pinned, you get bitten until you hit it or break free. Watch your back on broken ground.",home:"Broken ground, ledges and rocky forest.",stages:["Threads in the feathers around the neck.","Feathers falling out where the Graft veins run.","Growths through its back and arms. It watches longer before it moves.","Overgrown. It still climbs. It still waits behind you."]},triceratops:{about:"Three horns, a solid frill, and the weight of a truck. A plant-eater.",habits:"It grazes until you come within ten blocks or hurt it, then charges. The charge runs long and turns wide. Late in the Graft it charges anything that is not a dinosaur.",home:"Fern clearings and forest meadows.",stages:["Threads around the base of the horns.","Graft veins across the frill.","Growths have cracked the frill. It charges sooner.","Overgrown. It charges anything that moves."]},stegosaurus:{about:"Two rows of tall plates down its back and four spikes on the tail. Slow and calm.",habits:"It never charges. Anything behind it or at its sides gets the tail, and the tail is deadly. Pass in front of it, wide.",home:"Open conifer forest.",stages:["Threads between the plates.","Graft veins through the plates, dark at the edges.","Growths between the plates. The tail swings wider.","Overgrown. The plates rattle when it moves."]},ankylosaurus:{about:"Low, wide and covered in bone plates, with a club on the end of its tail.",habits:"It guards its ground. Come close and it turns its back and shuffles toward you, club first. The club throws you far. It breaks nothing but you.",home:"Dense lowland forest.",stages:["Threads in the gaps between the armour plates.","Graft veins under the armour.","Growths have pushed plates loose. The club swings harder.","Overgrown. The armour sits on top of the growth now."]},pachycephalosaurus:{about:"A thick dome of bone on its head, ringed with knobs. A plant-eater, about my size.",habits:"It charges with its head down when you are within a few blocks, from the side if it can. A hit staggers you and throws you back. It is fast but short-winded.",home:"Hills and rocky slopes.",stages:["Threads around the knobs of the dome.","Graft veins over the dome.","Growths through the dome. It charges more often.","Overgrown. The knock when it hits sounds hollow."]},baryonyx:{about:"A long-snouted hunter with a big hooked thumb claw. A fisher, like the sailed one, but it keeps to the shallows.",habits:"It stands still in shallow water and hooks at anything on the bank, then drags it toward the water. It never goes into deep water. Animals that come to drink do not come back.",home:"Shallow river banks and swamp edges.",stages:["Threads around the thumb claws.","Graft veins along the snout.","Growths through its back. It reaches further from the water.","Overgrown. The claws are buried in growth and still hook."]},dilophosaurus:{about:"A light hunter with two thin crests along the top of the skull. Fast and nimble, not big.",habits:"It darts in, bites, and darts back out, keeping its distance between bites. Hit it when it comes in, not when it backs off.",home:"Coasts and river mouths.",stages:["Threads along the crests.","Graft veins down the neck.","Growths through the crests. It bites faster.","Overgrown. The crests are lost in growth."]},parasaurolophus:{about:"A duck-billed plant-eater with a long hollow crest curving back from its head.",habits:"It does not run. Come near and it sounds the horn, the loudest sound in the basin, then rushes you and stamps down with its front feet. Every hunter that hears the horn comes toward you. If you hear it, move.",home:"Wetland forest and lakeshores.",stages:["Threads along the crest.","Graft veins through the crest. The call sounds rougher.","Growths have split the crest. The call carries further.","Overgrown. The horn never stops for long."]},therizinosaurus:{about:"Huge, pot-bellied and feathered, with claws as long as my arm. A plant-eater, of all things.",habits:"It guards a patch of forest. Step into its ring of trees and it rears up, then swings the claws. It does not chase far. Go around.",home:"A patch of broadleaf forest it guards.",stages:["Threads in the feathers along the arms.","Graft veins down the claws.","Growths through its back. It guards a wider ring.","Overgrown. It rears before you are even in the trees."]},brachiosaurus:{about:"The biggest animal I have ever seen. Its head is up in the treetops. The ground shakes when it walks.",habits:"It walks a long slow loop. Walk up to it and it rears and stamps. Stay out from under its feet. Late in the Graft the growths on its body open and let out small things that hunt.",home:"Open forest and river valleys.",stages:["Threads at the base of the neck.","Graft veins along the whole neck, from the ground to the treetops.","Pods on its back and sides. They open. Small things come out.","Overgrown. A walking hive."]},compsognathus:{about:"Tiny hunters, no bigger than a chicken, in small groups. The only animals here that come in numbers.",habits:"They follow anything that is hurt. One bite is nothing; a group adds up. Turn and hit one and the rest scatter. They clean up after the big hunters.",home:"Anywhere. They go where the blood is.",stages:["Threads in their mouths. All of them.","Graft veins under their skin. They follow closer.","Growths on their backs. They do not scatter as easily.","Overgrown. The whole group moves as one."]},microraptor:{about:"Tiny four-winged gliders, black-feathered, in small groups up in the canopy.",habits:"One is nothing. A group dives and pecks. Watch them more than fight them: when something big comes near, the whole group bursts out of the trees screaming. That is the warning.",home:"The forest canopy.",stages:["Threads in the wing feathers.","Graft veins along the wings. Patches of feathers gone.","Growths on their backs. They glide badly.","Overgrown. They still scatter when the big ones come."]}};export{i as C,d as J,h as a,l as b};
